I added the fallback, but **one thing needs your attention first:** the code had a comment saying the account-reference-only matching was deliberate, with "no phone/amount fallback (see Ledger)". I couldn't open that Ledger from this session, so I don't know why that decision was made. You asked me not to stop, so I built the fallback with a safety rule. Please check the Ledger, or with whoever made that call, before this ships.

**What changed**
- `matatu/payments/matcher.py`: a new function, `open_ticket_by_phone_and_amount`, picks the open ticket with the line's phone number and amount, **but only if exactly one such ticket exists.** A phone can hold up to 6 tickets, often at the same fare. If more than one matches, the line still goes to suspense rather than paying the wrong ticket.
- `matatu/payments/reconcile.py`: when the reference matches no ticket, reconciliation now tries that fallback before sending the line to suspense. Lines that still match nothing keep the reason "unknown reference". I replaced the old "no fallback" comment with one saying finance asked for this.
- `tests/test_reconcile_phone_fallback.py` (new) checks five cases:
  - a match on phone and amount succeeds;
  - already-paid tickets are ignored;
  - a different amount doesn't match;
  - two equal open tickets go to suspense;
  - a second identical payment doesn't land on a ticket the first one already paid.

**Test results:** all 11 tests pass. Before my change, one existing test was already failing: `test_mistyped_references_end_up_on_their_ticket`. It now passes, but only because each ticket in that test has its own phone number, so the new fallback catches it. The code still doesn't clean up mistyped references like "tkt 2345ab". If you want that properly fixed, it's a separate change to how references are matched.

